y into this and  particularly what happens with  electrical stimulation which is one of  the ways we have to modulate  dopaminergic function so we use a  technique called fixed potential and per  AMA tree and if you can see in the right  upper hand panel there are two  electrodes and there's a stimulating  electrode and a carbon-fibre electrode  and the carbon fiber electrode has its  potential clamped at 0.8 volts which is  near the oxidation potential of dopamine  and then any dopamine molecules that are  in the vicinity of electrode can attach  to it and they become oxidized and  donate to electrons and that can be  detected as a small current then we can  look very specifically at the effects of  stimulation and what it does to the  local dopamine transmission as you can  see in the lower panel that with brief  pulses of stimulation in white  matter above the caudate you don't see a  dopaminergic current and once you get in  the body of the caudate and the lower  two traces there is an actual  dopaminergic current and then next we  explored the differences between high  and low frequency stimulation and this  is best illustrated in the lower panel  so with low frequency stimulation at 20  Hertz you don't see much of a change in  the dopaminergic current there's a  little increase with each stimulus pulse  and then it relaxes back to normal if  you go into 200 Hertz that's the black  trace see there's actually a significant  increase in dopamine release that's  sustained for the period of the  stimulation which in this case is one  second so this gives us a mechanism  whereby we can potentially modulate the  vocal phasic levels of dopamine and  determine their effect on learning and  the real question is can we have time  stimulation that sort of mimics the  natural dopamine release and would that  modulate learning in any way so like I  showed you previously it's the same  circuitry and the idea is that the  stimulation is modulating and enhancing  the phasic dopamine levels so we  actually did these kinds of experiments  where the animals perform the same  associative learning tasks that I showed  you before but then during the feedback  period of correct trials they got  high-frequency stimulation so specific  stimulation occurring at a certain time  and after correct trials or as a control  after incorrect trials that's the basic  paradigm and at first we thought that  this was somehow I was just disrupt  learning or kind of screw it all up but  in fact what we found is that this type  of high-frequency stimulation actually  enhances learning so here's a segment of  the learning curve and control is in  black and then in blue is  the learning curve with stimulation you  can see is that the learning is better  it's it's faster than occurs in the  normal state and then we looked at high  frequency and low frequency stimulation  and we can only really see the effect  with high frequency stimulation so the  blue line is is higher than the black  line low frequency stimulation which is  a red line really didn't show the same  effect and interestingly unstimulated  trials in the same session didn't show  an effect so the effect wasn't sort of  spilling over or leaking over unto other  other trial types and then we looked at  stimulation following correct and  incorrect trials so again the baseline  learning curve is in black the blue line  represents learning with stimulation  after correct trials and so we see the  enhancement of learning and then the red  line shows what happens if we stimulate  after incorrect trials and here it's  very interesting because it seems that  stimulation after incorrect trials  actually retards learning it slows down  learning it says if the animal has to  sort of work its way around this  associate is inappropriate Association  to ultimately learn what needs to be  learned since then that was about ten  years ago we've repeated this experiment  and used combined stimulation in both  the caudate nucleus and the nucleus  accumbens with the idea that the caudate  nucleus is involved in learning and the  nucleus accumbens is involved in  motivation and what we found was that  combined stimulation actually leads to  very profound increase in learning so if  you look at the black line that's the  baseline and then the Green Line is the  stimulation and the caudate nucleus and  the red line is combined stimulation and  in this case we really get a very  significant effect both in terms of the  speed of learning and in the final  performance show not only the animals  faster but they're become more accurate  so we find that this was very  interesting and this is recently  published in 2016 so given what we've  learned here so far you know we can  reasonably conclude that the caudate  nucleus plays an important role in  learning that dopamine is potentially  important in reinforcing associations  that stimulation after correct trials  results in enhanced learning but  possibly due to hands dopamine release  where stimulation after incorrect trials  seems to [ __ ] alert and so this led us  to the question of could you use this  type of stimulation to enhance the  recovery or the learning after some type  of brain injury could you use this in  some viable clinical scenario so to do  this we created a mouse model of  traumatic brain injury this slide shows  a histology slide of the mouse brain and  as you can see the red hair there shows  the area that's been damaged by using a  controlled cortical impact and then this  is the model that we then used to  evaluate this potential treatment and  then the animals basically perform on  the Morris water maze so imagine that  the animal has the TBI the traumatic  brain injury and then they have an  electrode implanted and then they have  to swim around this maze as shown in the  upper right hand side so they swim from  the edge to a platform that's hidden  they can see it from the surface of the  water and there are visual cues on the  sides that indicate to them where to go  and the two lower panels show deliberate  electrolytic lesions that we made and  nucleus accumbens and the caudate  nucleus to confirm that we were in the  right place and we found is that there  was indeed an enhancement in their  learning on this water maze task so  these are different search patterns that  the animals had with and without  stimulation so the dashed blue  represents the court of animals that  no stimulation and you can see that even  by day five they're still sort of pretty  lost and they're swimming all over the  place whereas the red lion represents  animals that had stimulation after they  reach the platform and you can see they  have much more much shorter and much  more deliberate search paths and this  can be quantified as the escape latency  which is the time it takes them to find  the hidden platform which is very  similar to the learning curve which is  going in the opposite direction so in  this case that black line indicates the  normals the controls and they have a  gradual improvement in their escape  latency the red line represents the  injured animals that don't get  stimulation and so they do not perform  well and they continue to not perform  well and then the blue line represents  animals that had a brain injury and had  the stimulation and you can see they  have an improvement in their and their  learning and start to approach the  control animals we also wanted to test  if this effect was durable or if it was  sort of evanescent so in a subset of  these animals we stopped stimulation  after five days waited 10 days and then  tested them again at 15 days and what  you see is that there is a significant  retention of the learned material so the  black and the blue lines that is the  control and the stimulated animals  essentially started at the same point so  it's not that they forgotten or that  it's evanescent the effect is still  persistent and then ultimately the the  injured animals in the red line  eventually start to get better but they  never get quite as good as the as the  control animals or the animals that  received stimulation you also tested  this in humans so as part of a separate  project where we had electrodes  implanted into the brain for the  purposes of seizure localization we had  an opportunity to test three patients  and to determine whether we could see a  similar phenomenon and these results are  illustrated  here so this is this is the learning in  three different human subjects panels a  and B represent the learning curves  where the electrodes were close to the  caudate or in the caudate and the blue  line indicates the the presence of  stimulation and the red line indicates  that they were not stimulated and so on  both of these patients there was indeed  an effect of stimulation on on learning  as expected and predicted by the rodents  and the primate studies panel see shows  a subject in which the electrode was  closer to the putamen and there you  don't see a learning effect so it's very  specific to the caudate and this slide  summarizes these findings effectively  you've seen this learning enhancement  across three species now starting with  the mouse where we use the Morris water  maze and then going to the non-human  primate we've repeatedly shown this  enhancement of learning and then finally  in a limited number of human subjects  but still saw the same finding so this  gives us a lot of confidence that yeah  this is a real effect particularly that  we can find it across three species and  using different behavioral tasks so this  kind of brings us to the end of the talk  and so you know things that we can feel  comfortable about that the caudate and  the dorsal lateral prefrontal cortex  respond to unexpected positive outcomes  consistent with the reward prediction  error interestingly the caudate and  dorsal lateral prefrontal cortex neurons  also respond to unexpected negative  outcomes and so 