s syndrome could be  conceptualized as a maladaptive  Association similarly obsessions and  compulsions and obsessive compulsive  disorder can be looked at as maladaptive  associations and for instance triggers  for smoking or other types of substance  abuse can be looked at as maladaptive  associations and so the question is can  you  use stimulation in a deliberate fashion  to weaken these associations and  possibly replace them with other more  useful associations so my mind those are  some of the big questions but they are  you know of course many others to  consider so that concludes my talk and  thank you very much and I look forward  to answering any questions thank you dr.  s kandar for that informative  presentation we will now start the live  Q&amp;A portion of the webinar if you have a  question you'd like to ask please do so  now just click on the ask a question  drop-down box located on the far left of  your presentation window type your  question into the box that appears on  your screen  and click the send button will answer as  many of your questions as we have time  for let's get started this question is  how do you know the learning effect is  not due to reward so that's a really  good question that is how do you know  that the effect isn't simply perception  on the subjects part that this is just  an enhanced reward and I didn't show  this data but we actually looked at this  very carefully so we for instance did  experiments where the animal had a  choice between getting juice or getting  stimulation and 90 some percent of time  they selected the juice but also the  experiments were they had two conditions  one of just juice and one of juice plus  stimulation and they picked at about 50%  so that tells us that they don't  perceive the stimulation as in any way  rewarding or hedonic or positive and  it's well below the levels that have  historically been used for you know  intracranial soft stimulation so we're  operating and possibly up to one  milliamp and those older studies have  used all the way up to ten milliamps  so we really don't think it's somehow  enhanced reward effects well thank you  the next one is how specific is the  learning effect this is a really good  question and  I'll answer that in two parts one is we  haven't really fully tested if this  generalizes in any way she's relatively  specific when the question is where does  this specificity arise and I think it  arises from the circuitry kind of as I  showed in the beginning that is for any  given behavior there's a subset of  circuits that becomes activated and it's  that subset that then gets facilitated  if the behavior leads to unexpectedly  good outcome so the stimulation doesn't  have to be very specific  it's the circuits that are specific and  I think that's how we achieve this  effect it's still to be determined  whether it generalizes in some way to  other related behaviors the next one how  durable is the learning enhancement this  is also a really good question and you  know the best answer we have for that is  from the rodent experiments as you  recall in those experiments we stopped  stimulating and then evaluated animals  ten days later and the effect seems to  persist completely in that the  stimulated animals and the controls were  started at the same place that's as long  as we looked but that seems to suggest  that 10 days would be certainly long  enough to make a difference in someone's  rehabilitation or learning I would like  to once again thank dr. s Kamdar for his  presentation I would also like to thank  lab roots for making today's educational  webcast possible before we go I want to  let everyone know that today's webcast  will be available for on-demand viewing  through June 15th 2018 you will receive  an email from lab roots letting you know  when this webcast will be available for  replay please show that an announcement  with your colleagues who may miss  today's live event that is all for now  thank you fo